---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-brevo';
const title = 'Postscript vs Brevo for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Postscript vs Brevo for Shopify stores — SMS-native DTC specialization vs budget-friendly email-plus-SMS, GDPR-conscious stacks, and channel economics.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Postscript or Brevo better for Shopify SMS?',
    answer: 'Compare the actual Shopify SMS jobs rather than an attributed revenue threshold. In a proposed test, submit a keyword opt-in, recover an eligible cart, route a reply and cancel a queued promotion after purchase. Verify current destination support, channel-specific permission, suppression and sender ownership in each account; do not infer a winner from the product category alone.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Brevo replace Postscript for a Shopify store?',
    answer: 'Replacement depends on the required events, destinations, replies and recovery controls, not an arbitrary monthly message cutoff. Replay the same proposed cart and purchase events in both candidates, inspect queued work, and confirm that a cancellation reaches the system that owns the send. Treat unsupported jobs and current plan restrictions as open checks before migration.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use Brevo for email and Postscript for SMS?',
    answer: 'A split stack is a candidate design if one system owns email and another owns SMS. Document permission separately for each purpose and channel; email permission is not SMS permission. Share purchase and suppression changes, assign one owner per recovery job, and test cancellation and replay deduplication across both systems before switching traffic.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Postscript vs Brevo pricing compare?',
    answer: 'Request current quotes for the same twelve-month workload. Include email volume, SMS destinations and segments, carrier fees, number rental, credits and expiry, plan features, onboarding, integration upkeep and operator time. Use the same currency and tax basis, and price both a consolidated stack and any required email companion. A low entry price does not establish lower all-in cost.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Brevo or Postscript?',
    answer: 'Sequenzy offers pay-per-email lifecycle playbooks — pair with Postscript for US SMS depth or evaluate Brevo for combined email-SMS if budget is primary constraint. Sequenzy plus Postscript targets merchants who want guided email without Brevo\'s DIY automation builder. None replaces the other without tradeoffs on price vs specialization.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which is better for GDPR and EU Shopify merchants?',
    answer: 'Do not treat a vendor location, residency claim or compliance feature as proof that a merchant workflow is lawful. Confirm current processor terms, transfer arrangements, supported destinations and retention controls with the responsible reviewer. Separately test permission records by purpose and channel, withdrawal propagation and suppression at send time; keep unresolved legal and supplier facts on publication hold.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles cart abandonment better?',
    answer: 'Use a proposed cart fixture with an eligible shopper, a later purchase, a withdrawal and a replayed event. Check that the owning queue cancels stale recovery, reads current purchase and suppression state and avoids duplicate sends. Compare reply handling and delivery evidence separately from revenue attribution. A randomized eligible-audience holdout with an uncertainty interval is needed to estimate causal recovery lift.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Propose keyword opt-in, cart recovery with a reply, a quiet-hours broadcast and cross-channel cancellation tests. Record expected outcomes before running them, then inspect permissions, current purchase and subscription state, queue ownership, cancellation and replay deduplication. Compare matched all-in cost and operator effort; do not declare either platform the winner before evidence exists.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Postscript vs Brevo</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs Brevo for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          This comparison proposes Shopify email-plus-SMS tests for Postscript and Brevo. The merchant scenarios below are numeric fixtures, not observed accounts or measured outcomes. Current vendor capabilities, pricing and destination support require confirmation before publication or purchasing. Evaluate each channel alongside the email companion, integration work and operator time it needs.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'US SMS specialization', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Drop campaigns, TCPA guardrails, and conversational recovery.' },
            { label: 'Budget email + SMS', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Combined channel pricing for moderate volume merchants.' },
            { label: 'EU GDPR email home', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Familiar compliance patterns and transactional infrastructure.' },
            { label: 'Flash sale SMS', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Segmentation depth and two-way replies under sale pressure.' },
            { label: 'Also consider', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Guided email lifecycle if Brevo DIY automation stalls growth.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Postscript</th><th>Brevo</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>SMS-native Shopify DTC</td><td>Budget email + moderate SMS</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>Usage-based plans</td><td>Free tier; paid from ~$25/mo</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Billing model</td><td>Plan + per-message</td><td>Email volume / plan features + SMS credits</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email depth</td><td>Not included</td><td>Core product with automation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS depth</td><td>Best-in-class for Shopify US DTC</td><td>Functional for moderate volume</td></tr>
            <tr><td>GDPR / EU fit</td><td>US TCPA-first</td><td>Strong EU merchant default</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Two-way SMS</td><td>Core strength</td><td>Limited</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical role</td><td>US SMS channel owner</td><td>Consolidated ESP + light SMS</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What this comparison is actually about</h2>
        <p>
          Postscript and Brevo sit in the same Shopify email-tools category — newsletters, lifecycle automation, and transactional-style customer moments — but they punish different operational mistakes. Postscript is optimized when SMS is a primary revenue channel with compliance discipline. Brevo is optimized when cost per send matters more than predictive merchandising. Buyers who choose on brand recognition alone often end up paying for depth they never open or speed they outgrow in ninety days.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Before Essen release week: board game store retention choices</h2>
        <p>
          In a proposed numeric fixture, the board game store handles 1,050 orders/mo at roughly $67k/mo. The modeled revenue rhythm follows pre-order allocation anxiety on hot titles, which means email must handle pre-order state machines and fulfillment pivots after allocation closes before Essen release week — not after support tickets spike. Postscript contributes Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails; Brevo contributes affordable email volume with SMS and transactional options. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: pause winback until fulfillment tags show shipped for pre-orders.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed peak-week priority is operational updates within their permitted purpose, eligible cart recovery, welcome education, then promotions. Assign one sender and queue owner to each job. Test a purchase, withdrawal and subscription pause immediately before execution: refresh current state, cancel stale queued work and replay the same event to verify deduplication. Record expected outcomes before testing either platform; no preview result or platform advantage has been observed here.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed board game fixture, separate pre-order allocation updates from marketing permission. Exercise a partial allocation, a fulfillment change and a shipped tag before allowing winback; a replay must not create a second recovery job. Pair channels only after documenting the intended cooldown and testing cross-channel cancellation; forty-five minutes is a proposed fixture, not a guaranteed platform behavior.
        </p>
        <h2>Specialty Tea Merchant: 28–35 day tin replenishment with gift-box seasonality</h2>
        <p>
          In a proposed numeric fixture, the specialty tea merchant handles 720 orders/mo at roughly $44k/mo. The modeled revenue rhythm follows 28–35 day tin replenishment with gift-box seasonality, which means email must handle subscription skip handling and gift-buyer exclusions in December before Q4 gift-box peak — not after support tickets spike. Postscript contributes Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails; Brevo contributes affordable email volume with SMS and transactional options. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: split replenishment windows by blend and steeping rate.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed peak-week priority is operational updates within their permitted purpose, eligible cart recovery, welcome education, then promotions. Assign one sender and queue owner to each job. Test a purchase, withdrawal and subscription pause immediately before execution: refresh current state, cancel stale queued work and replay the same event to verify deduplication. Record expected outcomes before testing either platform; no preview result or platform advantage has been observed here.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed tea fixture, keep blend-specific replenishment clocks and distinguish a gift purchaser from the intended recipient. Exercise a subscription skip and a gift-box exclusion before the December campaign; neither should inherit permission from an order alone. Pair channels only after documenting the intended cooldown and testing cross-channel cancellation; forty-five minutes is a proposed fixture, not a guaranteed platform behavior.
        </p>
        <h2>How artisan coffee roaster emails through holiday blend drop</h2>
        <p>
          In a proposed numeric fixture, the artisan coffee roaster handles 860 orders/mo at roughly $52k/mo. The modeled revenue rhythm follows roast-date replenishment and wholesale sample follow-ups, which means email must handle bean reorder at day thirty with subscription pause confirmations before holiday blend drop — not after support tickets spike. Postscript contributes Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails; Brevo contributes affordable email volume with SMS and transactional options. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: suppress subscribers on pause from roast-date replenishment nudges.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed peak-week priority is operational updates within their permitted purpose, eligible cart recovery, welcome education, then promotions. Assign one sender and queue owner to each job. Test a purchase, withdrawal and subscription pause immediately before execution: refresh current state, cancel stale queued work and replay the same event to verify deduplication. Record expected outcomes before testing either platform; no preview result or platform advantage has been observed here.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed coffee fixture, use roast date and SKU rather than a customer-wide average. Exercise a paused subscription and a wholesale sample account, then confirm that retail replenishment does not bypass either exclusion. Pair channels only after documenting the intended cooldown and testing cross-channel cancellation; forty-five minutes is a proposed fixture, not a guaranteed platform behavior.
        </p>
        <h2>Climbing Gear Store ($89k/mo) — branch browse sequences by fall-rating and harness fit pages</h2>
        <p>
          In a proposed numeric fixture, the climbing gear store handles 1,100 orders/mo at roughly $89k/mo. The modeled revenue rhythm follows seven-to-fourteen day harness research cycles, which means email must handle certification education before percentage-off nudges on ropes before spring rack refresh — not after support tickets spike. Postscript contributes Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails; Brevo contributes affordable email volume with SMS and transactional options. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: branch browse sequences by fall-rating and harness fit pages.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed peak-week priority is operational updates within their permitted purpose, eligible cart recovery, welcome education, then promotions. Assign one sender and queue owner to each job. Test a purchase, withdrawal and subscription pause immediately before execution: refresh current state, cancel stale queued work and replay the same event to verify deduplication. Record expected outcomes before testing either platform; no preview result or platform advantage has been observed here.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed climbing fixture, branch harness-fit and fall-rating education by the actual product interest. Use approved product guidance, test a purchase before the discount step, and keep certification claims subject to manufacturer review; no margin improvement is asserted. Pair channels only after documenting the intended cooldown and testing cross-channel cancellation; forty-five minutes is a proposed fixture, not a guaranteed platform behavior.
        </p>
        <h2>Before Memorial Day camping spike: camping equipment merchant retention choices</h2>
        <p>
          In a proposed numeric fixture, the camping equipment merchant handles 1,350 orders/mo at roughly $78k/mo. The modeled revenue rhythm follows tent-capacity quizzes and family car-camping segments, which means email must handle trip-season browse recovery on mobile PDPs before cart before Memorial Day camping spike — not after support tickets spike. Postscript contributes Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails; Brevo contributes affordable email volume with SMS and transactional options. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: route quiz outcomes into different welcome branches before tent promos.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed peak-week priority is operational updates within their permitted purpose, eligible cart recovery, welcome education, then promotions. Assign one sender and queue owner to each job. Test a purchase, withdrawal and subscription pause immediately before execution: refresh current state, cancel stale queued work and replay the same event to verify deduplication. Record expected outcomes before testing either platform; no preview result or platform advantage has been observed here.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed camping fixture, route tent-capacity quiz outcomes into family or solo welcome branches. Test a changed quiz answer and a later tent purchase before mobile browse recovery so that a stale segment cannot trigger an irrelevant promotion. Pair channels only after documenting the intended cooldown and testing cross-channel cancellation; forty-five minutes is a proposed fixture, not a guaranteed platform behavior.
        </p>
        <h2>Pottery Studio: class waitlists and glaze-kit starter sets</h2>
        <p>
          In a proposed numeric fixture, the pottery studio handles 480 orders/mo at roughly $31k/mo. The modeled revenue rhythm follows class waitlists and glaze-kit starter sets, which means email must handle kiln-care post-purchase and holiday gift exclusions before holiday starter-set rush — not after support tickets spike. Postscript contributes Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails; Brevo contributes affordable email volume with SMS and transactional options. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: exclude class waitlist leads from glaze-kit promos after signup source tagging.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed peak-week priority is operational updates within their permitted purpose, eligible cart recovery, welcome education, then promotions. Assign one sender and queue owner to each job. Test a purchase, withdrawal and subscription pause immediately before execution: refresh current state, cancel stale queued work and replay the same event to verify deduplication. Record expected outcomes before testing either platform; no preview result or platform advantage has been observed here.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed pottery fixture, separate class-policy and kiln-safety education from glaze-kit promotions. Test a class-only signup and a kit buyer, with purpose-specific permission and current subscription state checked before either branch executes. Pair channels only after documenting the intended cooldown and testing cross-channel cancellation; forty-five minutes is a proposed fixture, not a guaranteed platform behavior.
        </p>
        <h2>Postscript workflow reality: strengths, friction, and billing</h2>
        <p>
          Evaluate SMS delivery, reply routing and quiet-hours behavior alongside the email system required for newsletters and lifecycle work. Proposed billing fixtures should include a limited drop, a replenishment cohort and a peak broadcast. Neither preview reliability nor support effort has been measured here.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed billing stress cases include a vinyl waitlist importing thousands of eligible contacts while orders stay flat and an outdoor retailer sending a May peak broadcast. These are workload fixtures, not observed invoices. Compare annual quotes for both with the same audience, destination mix and required companion services; do not assume one billing basis produces the opposite result.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Newsletter layer: editorial merchandising without discount training</h2>
        <p>
          Newsletters still matter. Coffee origin stories, vinyl staff picks, keyboard switch explainers, and climbing trip reports build trust before product blocks. Test product campaigns in the appropriate channel and include the required email companion. Change a hero SKU mid-week, sell out a limited run and inspect the next queued message against current inventory; do not presume either integration refreshes it automatically.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Pricing models that punish different mistakes</h2>
        <p>
          Use 12,000 and 18,000 contacts as proposed workload fixtures, not price-convergence thresholds. Quote the same eligible audiences, email sends and SMS destinations over twelve months. Include carrier fees, message segments, numbers, expired credits, required companion tools, migration and operator time on the same currency and tax basis. No invoice outcome is claimed.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Hybrid stack patterns on Shopify</h2>
        <p>
          Common pattern: capture in Privy or Justuno, lifecycle in Brevo, SMS in Postscript when compliance matters. Failure mode: three cart recovery senders with no priority order. Pick a primary lifecycle owner.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Postscript if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Sms is a primary revenue channel with compliance discipline</li>
          <li>You will use Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails weekly</li>
          <li>Message volume and plans fits your calendar</li>
          <li>Your team can maintain Postscript without external rescue</li>
          <li>Switching cost favors Postscript for the next twelve months</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose Brevo if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Cost per send matters more than predictive merchandising</li>
          <li>You will use affordable email volume with SMS and transactional options weekly</li>
          <li>Email volume and features fits your calendar</li>
          <li>Your team can maintain Brevo without external rescue</li>
          <li>A randomized eligible-audience holdout estimates incremental margin with an uncertainty interval sufficient to assess migration cost</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Treat Postscript as an SMS candidate alongside an email companion and Brevo as a candidate for coordinated channel work, subject to current capability checks. Attributed sales describe reporting rules, not causal lift: use a randomized eligible-audience holdout and report uncertainty before claiming incremental margin. Run the five-job buyer test in both UIs, model twelve-month billing with real sends and list hygiene, and pick whichever platform lets you edit audiences safely the Thursday before a drop — not whichever sales deck had the prettier logos. See <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing models</a> before annual contracts.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-mailchimp">Brevo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-omnisend">Brevo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp">Postscript vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for">Browse store-type guides</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs Brevo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
